# Architecture (Quizmatik / Godot 4.7)

Math-quiz arcade shooter. Prefer the smallest change that fits existing patterns. Do not invent parallel systems.

Godot 4.x only: `@export`/`@onready`, `signal.connect(cb)`, `instantiate()`, typed GDScript, `Input` actions.

## Target platforms (critical)

Ship and play well on **all three**: **Mobile** (Android/iOS), **Steam** (desktop), **HTML5** (web).

- Design every feature/control/UI for comfortable play on touch **and** mouse/keyboard — not PC-first with mobile as afterthought.
- Prefer InputMap actions (`shoot`, `ui_*`) over hard-coded keys; add touch controls (on-screen buttons / gestures) without breaking desktop/web.
- UI: readable at phone landscape, safe areas, hit targets large enough for fingers; still clean on 16:9 desktop and browser.
- Performance: mobile renderer / mid-range phones and browsers matter — avoid heavy one-platform-only effects unless gated.
- Platform-specific code: isolate behind small helpers/`OS.has_feature(...)` — do not fork gameplay into three codepaths.
- Before merging a feature, ask: *playable with touch, with keyboard/mouse, and in browser?*

## #1 Scene isolation (critical)

**Every scene must open and run alone in the editor without crashes**, with no parent context required.

- Bake safe defaults into **Resource files** (`.tres`) and `@export` them on the scene — enough to boot, play, and not null-ref.
- Parent contexts do **not** inject required nodes at edit-time. They **override** those resource defaults at runtime via events / `initialize(data)`.
- Typical override path: parent emits event → child `initialize` → `ResourceUtils.update_resource(live_resource, incoming)` (see `GameContext` + `game_config.tres`).
- Standalone defaults are valid fallbacks; full-app data always wins when the parent provides it.
- Never assume “only runs under `MainContext`”: no required autoloads, no required sibling contexts, no crash if event payload is empty (keep resource defaults).
- When adding a dependency, ask: *can this scene still F6/run isolated?* If not, put a default Resource on the scene.

## Entry & folders

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| `main.tscn` → `MainContext` | App root / context switcher |
| `src/contexts/` | Screens / orchestration: `main_`, `menu_`, `game_`, `post_battle_` |
| `src/features/` | Isolated gameplay features (own almost all deps) |
| `src/common/` | Project-shared Resources (`GameConfig`, `LevelsConfig`, settings) |
| `src/levels/{container}/` | Campaign `level_N.tres` (canonical) |
| `core/` | Project-agnostic code only (no Quizmatik types/scenes) |
| `tools/` | Offline generators (e.g. levels) |
| `src/dev/` | Non-runtime utilities — not the game entry |

Ignore for gameplay work: `addons/`, `.godot/`, `bin/`, legacy `src/levels/level_*.tres` (root).

## Layer contracts

### `src/features/` — isolated features
- One folder = one feature (`plane`, `answer`, `shot`, `explosion`, …).
- Keep **almost all dependencies inside** the feature: scripts, scenes, local helpers, feature-specific assets refs.
- Talk to the outside via a **small public surface** (signals `ev_*`, `initialize`/`setup`, exported PackedScenes).
- Features must **not** import other features' internals or context scripts.
- Contexts **compose/spawn** features; they do not reimplement feature logic.

### `core/` — project-agnostic
- May be reused in any Godot project: FSM, `EventListener`, shackers, window stack, ResourceUtils, etc.
- **Must not** depend on `src/` (no `GameConfig`, contexts, features, quiz types, project scenes).
- If code needs Quizmatik types → it belongs in `src/`, not `core/`.

### `src/contexts/` — orchestration only
- Wire features + shared config; own flow/FSM/UI for that screen.
- Do not dump reusable feature guts here — extract to `src/features/` or (if generic) `core/`.
- Each context scene ships its own default Resources; parents override them through `MainEvents` / `initialize(data)`, never by requiring the parent to be open in the editor.

## UI — editor-first (critical)

- Build and change UI **in the Godot editor** (nodes in `.tscn`), not by constructing controls in GDScript.
- Scripts only: bind `@export` node refs, update texts/visibility/state, connect signals.
- Need a repeated item (level button, row)? Create a **template `.tscn` in the editor**, then `instantiate()` — do not `Control.new()` + build the tree in code.
- Prefer editing the scene file / asking for editor steps over large programmatic UI diffs.
- Exception: tiny debug overlays or one-off runtime labels — still prefer a scene node when practical.
- **Theme in editor:** Project Settings `gui/theme/custom` applies at runtime, but isolated scene preview often shows the default theme. Assign `core/theme/style.tres` to the root `Control` of each UI window (`theme = ...`) so the editor matches the game.

## Context flow

```
Menu --ev_start_game--> Game --ev_exit_game--> PostBattle
PostBattle --ev_return_to_menu--> Menu
PostBattle --ev_start_game--> Game (retry)
```

- New screens: implement `IContext` (`initialize` / `deinit`), switch **only** via `MainEvents`.
- App-level signals: `MainEvents`. In-battle: `GameEvents`. No cross-context node refs.
- Subscribe with `EventListener`; always `deinit()` on teardown.
- Battle payload: **either** `custom_battle: GameConfig` **or** `battle_info: GameConfig.BattleInfo` — never both.

## Battle loop

`GameManager` FSM: `CountDown` → `Game` → `EndGame` (via `FSMGameEvents`).
Round logic: `RoundController` + `AnswerSpawner` + `CombatResolver` + `QuizQuestion`.
Do not drive win/lose with ad-hoc flags outside the FSM.

## Placement rules

- New isolated gameplay unit → `src/features/{name}/` (self-contained; `.gd` + `.tscn` colocated)
- New screen / flow → `src/contexts/{name}_context/`
- Project-shared Resource/type → `src/common/`
- Generic reusable utility (no project deps) → `core/lib/`
- Level data → `src/levels/{container}/`; metadata → `levels_config.gd` / `tools/generate_levels.py`
- Progress mutations → `ProgressController` only (`user://progress.tres`)

## Cheap-change heuristics

1. Match nearest existing file; copy its wiring (`@export`, signals `ev_*`).
2. Prefer editing one layer: data (`.tres`) → feature → context orchestration.
3. Before adding to `core/`: confirm zero `src/` imports. Before splitting a feature: keep deps inside it.
4. New scene dependency → default `.tres` on the scene + optional runtime override from parent event.
5. Do not rename entrenched typos (`persisten_*`, `fsm game_states.gd`) unless asked.
6. Do not edit `addons/` or binary assets; reference `res://` paths.
7. Live context Resources (e.g. `game_config.tres`) are defaults/overridden at runtime — change level `.tres` or `GameConfig` schema for real content.
